it's been an extraordinary week in so  many ways I think first of all the  great loss to this community has  suffered has in many ways been  related to and it has resonated not just  throughout Ireland but through the Irish  people abroad and  the messages that have come have been  very important to the community but  as I listened to  for the John Joe Duffy  whom I think uh Bishop Allen mcguckian  has referred to as a rock in the  community what was extraordinary  was listening to the long detail  of the lives  of the people their different  relationships to the community the  different tasks they did people who  worked with their hands and with their  head and all of their [ __ ] of what they  shared and it reminded me of what I had  said at the very beginning in a small  community where the relationships are so  close and where people have intimate  knowledge of families and circumstances  it isn't a single person or a family  that carries the big lash of a terrible  tragedy like this but it is the whole  community  but this watch has been very moving I  must say it's president of I've been  here and and being able to take so much  of the texture of this community in all  its different manifestations and  circumstances and has been very very  very very moving I think what will  happen now is that the first reaction  was very much  of shock and of a kind of silence and  anomalous  how you to handle a tragedy of this  magnitude  and then after that there was what had  to be done in pushing things together  and making all of the arrangements and  an extraordinary cooperation came out of  daesh and then there were the memories  and the tracings of all the different  patterns of what they had shared with  different people right up to this very  last they had a five-year-old lovely  girl and her father and I I think it  showed me something very much about it  is is this how the best instincts and  the decency that people come to the  floor when they're able to share  something like this and that was I think  what will happen now is that will be the  big challenge I think is that the  funerals are over but all the vacant  spaces out there and all the losses that  were experienced and people remember and  something new will have come in its  place new relationships new connections  new functions that will happen in the  community but I'm certain that they have  all of the ingredients of the courage  and the determination to make that  something new fresh and be as valuable  as what to have lost
 

